## Streaming Large Uploads in ASP.NET Core 8 Minimal API

`IFormFile` buffers the entire request into memory by default. For large files, read directly from `Request.Body` or use `Request.ReadFormAsync` with streaming options.

### Approach: Read the multipart body manually

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.WebUtilities;
using Microsoft.Net.Http.Headers;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// Raise the max request body size (default is ~28MB)
builder.WebHost.ConfigureKestrel(options =>
{
    options.Limits.MaxRequestBodySize = 2L * 1024 * 1024 * 1024; // 2 GB
});

var app = builder.Build();

app.MapPost("/upload", async (HttpRequest request) =>
{
    if (!request.HasFormContentType)
        return Results.BadRequest("Expected a multipart/form-data request.");

    var boundary = HeaderUtilities.RemoveQuotes(
        MediaTypeHeaderValue.Parse(request.ContentType).Boundary).Value;

    if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(boundary))
        return Results.BadRequest("Missing boundary.");

    var reader = new MultipartReader(boundary, request.Body);
    MultipartSection? section;

    while ((section = await reader.ReadNextSectionAsync()) is not null)
    {
        if (!ContentDispositionHeaderValue.TryParse(section.ContentDisposition, out var cd)
            || !cd.IsFileDisposition())
        {
            continue;
        }

        // Sanitize the file name
        var fileName = Path.GetRandomFileName() + Path.GetExtension(
            cd.FileName.Value);
        var savePath = Path.Combine("/tmp/uploads", fileName);

        Directory.CreateDirectory("/tmp/uploads");

        // Stream directly to disk — never holds the full file in memory
        await using var targetStream = File.Create(savePath);
        await section.Body.CopyToAsync(targetStream);
    }

    return Results.Ok("Upload complete.");
})
.DisableAntiforgery(); // required for minimal APIs accepting form data without a token

app.Run();
```

### Key configuration points

| Setting | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| `MaxRequestBodySize` | Kestrel rejects requests over 28 MB by default |
| `[RequestSizeLimit]` or `DisableRequestSizeLimit` | Per-endpoint override (for controller-based APIs) |
| `MultipartBodyLengthLimit` | If using `ReadFormAsync`, raise this in `FormOptions` |

### Why this works

- **`MultipartReader`** reads the raw stream section-by-section with a small internal buffer (~16 KB).
- **`CopyToAsync`** pipes each section's body straight to a `FileStream` — memory usage stays flat regardless of file size.
- You never call `Request.Form` or bind `IFormFile`, so ASP.NET never materializes the file in RAM.

### Optional: progress / cancellation

```csharp
var buffer = new byte[64 * 1024]; // 64 KB chunks
long totalRead = 0;
int bytesRead;

while ((bytesRead = await section.Body.ReadAsync(buffer, cancellationToken)) > 0)
{
    await targetStream.WriteAsync(buffer.AsMemory(0, bytesRead), cancellationToken);
    totalRead += bytesRead;
    // report progress, enforce custom size limits, etc.
}
```

### Don't forget

- **Disable request buffering** if you've enabled it globally (`request.EnableBuffering()` — avoid this for upload endpoints).
- **Reverse proxies** (nginx, IIS, Azure App Service) have their own body-size limits — configure those too (e.g., `client_max_body_size 2g;` in nginx, `maxAllowedContentLength` in `web.config`).
